<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maui</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maui is the second-largest island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, formed by two volcanoes joined by an isthmus, with the summit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haleakala">Haleakalā</a> in the east. Its chiefs, united under Piʻilani around 1500, became a leading power under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahekili-ii">Kahekili II</a> in the late 18th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> conquered the island in 1790 and 1795, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahaina">Lahaina</a> on its west coast served as the royal capital of the Kingdom of Hawaii from 1820 to 1845.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maui.jpg" alt="Maui" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maui_Landsat_Photo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Maui is the second-largest island in the Hawaiian archipelago, with an area of 1,883 square kilometres. Once part of Maui Nui, it is dominated by two volcanic features, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haleakala">Haleakalā</a> in the southeast and the West Maui Mountains in the northwest, joined by an isthmus about six miles wide that gives the island its nickname, the Valley Isle. Like the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaiian Islands</a> it was formed by volcanism associated with the Hawaii hotspot, and is a volcanic doublet, made of two shield volcanoes that overlapped. In Native Hawaiian tradition the navigator Hawaiʻiloa named the island after his son, who was himself named for the demigod Māui; its earlier name was ʻIhikapalaumaewa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> from Tahiti were the original inhabitants, and they introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">kapu</a> system, a strict social order that affected all aspects of life and became the core of Hawaiian culture. The island's central position gave it a pivotal role in Hawaiian history. In 1790 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>, king of Hawaiʻi island, invaded Maui and fought the inconclusive Battle of Kepaniwai. He returned to Hawaiʻi to fight a rival and subdued Maui a few years later.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> sighted Maui on 26 November 1778 but found no suitable landing, and the first European ashore was the Comte de Lapérouse, on 29 May 1786 at what became La Perouse Bay. Traders, whalers, loggers of sandalwood and missionaries followed. The missionaries, who began arriving from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> in 1823, settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahaina">Lahaina</a>, then the Hawaiian capital. They devised the Hawaiian alphabet to translate a Bible, ran a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press in Lahaina and began recording history that had been passed down orally. They opened the Lahainaluna Mission School in 1831, which still exists.</p><p>During the height of the whaling era, from 1843 to 1860, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahaina">Lahaina</a> was a major centre: in one season over 400 ships visited, with up to 100 anchored at once. Whaling declined steeply at the end of the 19th century as petroleum replaced whale oil. Maui was part of the Hawaiian Kingdom, the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, the Hawaiian territory and then the state of Hawaii, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, in 1943–1944, over 100,000 soldiers were based there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tourism is the island's major industry, and agriculture has long included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and pineapple. Sugar production ended in 2016. Haleakalā National Park, Hāna and the Hāna Highway, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iao-valley">ʻĪao Valley</a> are among its principal sights. The summit of Haleakalā has an observatory, the first astronomical facility in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. In August 2023 wildfires destroyed much of the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahaina">Lahaina</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late eighteenth century the Hawaiian Islands were divided among rival paramount chiefs, and Kahekili II of <b>Maui</b> had extended his rule over Oʻahu and Molokaʻi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Kalaniʻōpuʻu’s death in 1782 Kamehameha I defeated his heir, conquered <b>Maui</b>, Molokaʻi and, at the Battle of Nuʻuanu in 1795, Oʻahu, and by 1810 ruled all the islands as king.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>…the land again, and the chiefs who backed Kamehameha after 1782 took the lands of the defeated: his ally Keʻeaumoku Pāpaʻiahiahi, once a fugitive, became governor of <b>Maui</b>, and Kalanimoku, a young chief captured on the losing side at Mokuʻōhai, rose to be Kamehameha’s chief minister and treasurer.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li><li><blockquote>Then come the lineages of Wākea, whose son Hāloa is the ancestor of all people, of Papahānaumoku, of Māui and his adventures, and finally forty-four generations down to Piʻilani, ruler of <b>Maui</b>, and on to the late 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kumulipo">Kumulipo</a></li><li><blockquote>On his second visit to the Hawaiian Islands, which he called the Sandwich Islands, James Cook sighted <b>Maui</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-26">26 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Kaʻeokulani, who ruled Kauaʻi and for a time <b>Maui</b>, died in 1794, leaving the island to his young son Kaʻumualiʻi.</blockquote><a href="/state/chiefdom-of-kauai">Chiefdom of Kauai</a></li></ul></section>
